text:
Metric
What it measures
Watch out for




Absolute abundance (\(N_i\))
Number of individuals of a species
Depends on sampling effort. Only compare surveys with the same effort


Relative abundance (\(p_i\))
Proportion of all individuals that belong to a species
Hides differences in total abundance


Density (\(d_i\))
Individuals per unit area or effort
Needs an accurate measure of area or effort


Species richness (\(S\))
Number of species
Increases with sampling effort and is sensitive to rare species


Rarefied richness
Richness standardised to the same number of individuals
Throws away information when samples differ a lot in size


Shannon index (\(H'\))
Richness and evenness together
Hard to interpret in real-world terms. Moderately sensitive to rare species


Simpson index (\(D\))
Mostly evenness and dominance
Not very sensitive to rare species







App: richness, evenness and diversity



Code
import {fmt, red, grey} from "./apps/ecs200.js"











Code
viewof dv_s = Inputs.range([1, 12], {value: 5, step: 1, label: "Number of species (richness, S)"})
viewof dv_dom = Inputs.range([0, 0.95], {value: 0, step: 0.05, label: "Dominance of the most common species (0 = perfectly even)"})




















Code
dv = {
  // the first species takes a share set by the slider; the rest is split evenly
  const s = dv_s;
  let p;
  if (s === 1) p = [1];
  else {
    const first = 1 / s + dv_dom * (1 - 1 / s);
    p = [first, ...Array(s - 1).fill((1 - first) / (s - 1))];
  }
  const rows = p.map((v, i) =&gt; ({species: `sp${i + 1}`, p: v}));
  const H = -p.filter((v) =&gt; v &gt; 0).reduce((a, v) =&gt; a + v * Math.log(v), 0);
  const simpson = 1 - p.reduce((a, v) =&gt; a + v * v, 0);
  const inv = 1 / p.reduce((a, v) =&gt; a + v * v, 0);
  return {rows, H, simpson, inv, Hmax: Math.log(s)};
}

html`&lt;div&gt;${Plot.plot({
  width: 560, height: 250, marginLeft: 45,
  x: {label: null, domain: dv.rows.map((d) =&gt; d.species)},
  y: {label: "Relative abundance (pᵢ)", domain: [0, 1], grid: true},
  marks: [Plot.barY(dv.rows, {x: "species", y: "p", fill: (d, i) =&gt; (i === 0 ? red : grey)}), Plot.ruleY([0])]
})}
&lt;div class="app-verdict"&gt;Shannon H′ = &lt;b&gt;${fmt(dv.H)}&lt;/b&gt; (the maximum possible for ${dv_s} species is ln(S) = ${fmt(dv.Hmax)}) ·
Simpson's 1 − D = &lt;b&gt;${fmt(dv.simpson)}&lt;/b&gt; · Inverse Simpson = &lt;b&gt;${fmt(dv.inv)}&lt;/b&gt; "effective species".
Both indices are highest when all species are equally common, and fall as one species dominates, even though richness stays the same.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;`

1. Background
A two-sample t-test compares the means of two independent groups. It calculates a t value:
\[
t = \frac{\text{difference between the two group means}}{\text{standard error of that difference}} = \frac{\text{signal}}{\text{noise}}
\]
A large t (a big difference relative to the variation within groups) gives a small p-value. Three things make a difference easier to detect:

a bigger difference between the means (more signal);
less variation within each group (less noise);
a larger sample size (the standard error shrinks as n grows).

R uses Welch’s t-test by default. It does not assume the two groups have equal variances, so it is a safe default for ecological data.
Use the app below to see how these three ingredients change the result. Every time you move a slider, a new experiment is simulated in your browser.


App: signal versus noise in a t-test





Code
import {rng, rnorm, mean, sd, welch, qt975, fmt, fmtp, red, grey, dark} from "./apps/ecs200.js"









Code
viewof tt_diff = Inputs.range([0, 1], {value: 0.4, step: 0.05, label: "True difference between means (m/s)"})
viewof tt_sd = Inputs.range([0.05, 0.8], {value: 0.3, step: 0.05, label: "Variation within groups (SD)"})
viewof tt_n = Inputs.range([3, 50], {value: 10, step: 1, label: "Lizards per group (n)"})
viewof tt_seed = Inputs.button("Resample")


































Code
tt_data = {
  const r = rng(101 + tt_seed);
  const a = rnorm(r, tt_n, 1.2, tt_sd);
  const b = rnorm(r, tt_n, 1.2 + tt_diff, tt_sd);
  const pts = [
    ...a.map((v) =&gt; ({group: "20 °C", x: 1 + (r() - 0.5) * 0.25, y: v})),
    ...b.map((v) =&gt; ({group: "28 °C", x: 2 + (r() - 0.5) * 0.25, y: v}))
  ];
  const summ = [[a, 1], [b, 2]].map(([g, x]) =&gt; {
    const m = mean(g), half = qt975(g.length - 1) * sd(g) / Math.sqrt(g.length);
    return {x: x + 0.3, m, lo: m - half, hi: m + half};
  });
  return {a, b, pts, summ, test: welch(a, b)};
}

Plot.plot({
  width: 560, height: 300, marginLeft: 50,
  x: {domain: [0.5, 2.7], ticks: [1, 2], tickFormat: (d) =&gt; (d === 1 ? "20 °C" : "28 °C"), label: null},
  y: {label: "Sprint speed (m/s)", grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.dot(tt_data.pts, {x: "x", y: "y", fill: grey, fillOpacity: 0.6, r: 3.5}),
    Plot.ruleX(tt_data.summ, {x: "x", y1: "lo", y2: "hi", stroke: red, strokeWidth: 2.5}),
    Plot.dot(tt_data.summ, {x: "x", y: "m", fill: red, r: 6})
  ]
})
















Code
html`&lt;div class="app-verdict"&gt;Welch's &lt;i&gt;t&lt;/i&gt; = &lt;b&gt;${fmt(tt_data.test.t)}&lt;/b&gt;, df = ${fmt(tt_data.test.df, 1)}, &lt;i&gt;p&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;${fmtp(tt_data.test.p)}&lt;/b&gt;.
${tt_data.test.p &lt; 0.05
  ? "The difference is &lt;b&gt;statistically significant&lt;/b&gt;: it is large compared with the noise."
  : "The difference is &lt;b&gt;not statistically significant&lt;/b&gt;: we cannot separate it from random noise with this sample."}
Red points and bars show each group's mean and 95% confidence interval.&lt;/div&gt;`












Try this: set the true difference to 0 and press Resample many times. About 1 in 20 experiments will still be “significant” just by chance. That is what a significance level of α = 0.05 means.
Hypotheses:

Null hypothesis (H0): mean sprint speed is the same at 20 °C and 28 °C.
Alternative hypothesis (H1): mean sprint speed differs between 20 °C and 28 °C.
Significance level: α = 0.05.

1. Background
With three groups you could run three t-tests (20 vs 28, 20 vs 36, 28 vs 36), but every extra test adds another 5% chance of a false positive. A one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) asks a single question first: do all groups share the same mean, or does at least one differ?
ANOVA compares two kinds of variation:

Between-group variation: how far the group means are from the overall mean (signal).
Within-group variation: how spread out individuals are inside each group (noise).

\[
F = \frac{\text{variation between groups}}{\text{variation within groups}}
\]
A large F means the group means are further apart than the noise would suggest. If the ANOVA is significant, a post hoc test (Tukey’s HSD) then tells you which groups differ, while keeping the overall false-positive rate at 5%.


App: between- versus within-group variation





Code
import {anova1} from "./apps/ecs200.js"









Code
viewof an_spread = Inputs.range([0, 0.6], {value: 0.3, step: 0.05, label: "Spread of the group means (signal)"})
viewof an_sd = Inputs.range([0.05, 0.8], {value: 0.3, step: 0.05, label: "Variation within groups (noise)"})
viewof an_n = Inputs.range([3, 30], {value: 12, step: 1, label: "Lizards per group (n)"})
viewof an_seed = Inputs.button("Resample")


































Code
an_data = {
  const r = rng(202 + an_seed);
  const mus = [1.6 - an_spread, 1.6 + an_spread, 1.6];
  const labels = ["20 °C", "28 °C", "36 °C"];
  const groups = mus.map((m) =&gt; rnorm(r, an_n, m, an_sd));
  const pts = groups.flatMap((g, i) =&gt; g.map((v) =&gt; ({x: i + 1 + (r() - 0.5) * 0.3, y: v, group: labels[i]})));
  const means = groups.map((g, i) =&gt; ({x1: i + 0.7, x2: i + 1.3, m: mean(g)}));
  return {pts, means, grand: mean(groups.flat()), res: anova1(groups)};
}

Plot.plot({
  width: 560, height: 350, marginLeft: 50,
  x: {domain: [0.5, 3.5], ticks: [1, 2, 3], tickFormat: (d) =&gt; ["20 °C", "28 °C", "36 °C"][d - 1], label: null},
  y: {label: "Sprint speed (m/s)", grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleY([an_data.grand], {stroke: dark, strokeDasharray: "5,4"}),
    Plot.dot(an_data.pts, {x: "x", y: "y", fill: grey, fillOpacity: 0.6, r: 3}),
    Plot.link(an_data.means, {x1: "x1", x2: "x2", y1: "m", y2: "m", stroke: red, strokeWidth: 4})
  ]
})
















Code
html`&lt;div class="app-verdict"&gt;One-way ANOVA: &lt;i&gt;F&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;${an_data.res.df1},${an_data.res.df2}&lt;/sub&gt; = &lt;b&gt;${fmt(an_data.res.F)}&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;i&gt;p&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;${fmtp(an_data.res.p)}&lt;/b&gt;.
Red lines are the group means and the dashed line is the overall (grand) mean. ${an_data.res.p &lt; 0.05 ? "At least one group mean differs." : "No evidence that the group means differ."}&lt;/div&gt;`












Hypotheses:

H0: mean sprint speed is the same at all three temperatures.
H1: at least one temperature has a different mean sprint speed.

1. Background
A two-way ANOVA tests two categorical predictors at once (here temperature and population) and, most importantly, their interaction. Three questions are tested:

Main effect of temperature: averaged over both populations, does speed change with temperature?
Main effect of population: averaged over all temperatures, is one population faster?
Interaction (temperature × population): does the effect of temperature depend on which population the lizard came from?

If there is no interaction, the lines on an interaction plot (mean of each group joined by population) are parallel. If the lines are not parallel or they cross, the effect of one factor depends on the other. Use the app to build some interaction plots.


App: what does an interaction look like?





Code
viewof ix_temp = Inputs.range([-0.4, 0.4], {value: 0.25, step: 0.05, label: "Temperature effect (change per step: 20 → 28 → 36 °C)"})
viewof ix_pop = Inputs.range([-0.4, 0.4], {value: 0.1, step: 0.05, label: "Population effect (inland minus coastal)"})
viewof ix_int = Inputs.range([-0.4, 0.4], {value: 0, step: 0.05, label: "Interaction (extra temperature effect for inland lizards)"})



























Code
ix_means = {
  const temps = ["20 °C", "28 °C", "36 °C"];
  const out = [];
  temps.forEach((t, i) =&gt; {
    out.push({temp: t, pop: "coastal", mean: 1.4 + ix_temp * i});
    out.push({temp: t, pop: "inland", mean: 1.4 + ix_pop + (ix_temp + ix_int) * i});
  });
  return out;
}

Plot.plot({
  width: 560, height: 300, marginLeft: 50,
  x: {domain: ["20 °C", "28 °C", "36 °C"], label: "Test temperature", padding: 0.3},
  y: {label: "Mean sprint speed (m/s)", grid: true, domain: [0.3, 2.6]},
  color: {domain: ["coastal", "inland"], range: [grey, red], legend: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.line(ix_means, {x: "temp", y: "mean", stroke: "pop", strokeWidth: 3}),
    Plot.dot(ix_means, {x: "temp", y: "mean", fill: "pop", r: 6})
  ]
})
















Code
html`&lt;div class="app-verdict"&gt;${ix_int === 0
  ? "Interaction = 0, so the lines are &lt;b&gt;parallel&lt;/b&gt;: temperature has the same effect in both populations and you can interpret each main effect on its own."
  : "Interaction ≠ 0, so the lines are &lt;b&gt;not parallel&lt;/b&gt;: the effect of temperature depends on the population. Interpret the interaction first, not the main effects on their own."}&lt;/div&gt;`















A visual explanation of interactive effects.

1. Background
A simple linear regression fits the straight line that best describes how a numeric response (y) changes with a numeric predictor (x):
\[
y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i + \varepsilon_i
\]

\(\beta_0\) is the intercept: the predicted y when x = 0.
\(\beta_1\) is the slope: how much y changes, on average, for every one-unit increase in x. This is usually the number your research question is about.
\(\varepsilon_i\) is the residual: the vertical distance between each point and the line. Residuals are assumed to be normally distributed with constant spread.

R finds the line that makes the sum of the squared residuals as small as possible (least squares). The null hypothesis is that the slope is zero (no relationship).
A straight line is only a good summary if the relationship really is straight. In the app below, choose a pattern and compare the diagnostic plots with the ideal: a flat, patternless cloud in the residuals plot and points along the line in the Q-Q plot.


App: is a straight line a good fit?





Code
import {rng, rnorm, runif, mean, sd, lm1, lm2, cor, qqpoints, smooth, fmt, fmtp, red, grey, dark} from "./apps/ecs200.js"









Code
viewof lin_type = Inputs.radio(
  new Map([["Linear, going up", "up"], ["Linear, going down", "down"], ["Curved", "curve"], ["Fan-shaped", "fan"], ["One extreme outlier", "outlier"]]),
  {value: "up", label: "Pattern in the data"})
viewof lin_resid = Inputs.toggle({label: "Show residuals", value: false})
viewof lin_seed = Inputs.button("Resample")



























Code
lin_data = {
  const r = rng(707 + lin_seed);
  const n = 80;
  const x = runif(r, n, 0, 4);
  const e = rnorm(r, n, 0, 1);
  let y;
  if (lin_type === "up") y = x.map((v, i) =&gt; 1 + 1.5 * v + e[i]);
  if (lin_type === "down") y = x.map((v, i) =&gt; 8 - 1.5 * v + e[i]);
  if (lin_type === "curve") y = x.map((v, i) =&gt; 1 + 0.6 * v ** 3 + 2 * e[i]);
  if (lin_type === "fan") y = x.map((v, i) =&gt; 2 + 1.2 * v + e[i] * (0.2 + 1.1 * v));
  if (lin_type === "outlier") { y = x.map((v, i) =&gt; 2 + 0.3 * v + 0.8 * e[i]); x[0] = 3.9; y[0] = 14; }
  const fit = lm1(x, y);
  const pts = x.map((v, i) =&gt; ({x: v, y: y[i], fitted: fit.fitted[i], resid: fit.resid[i], std: fit.stdres[i]}));
  return {pts, fit};
}

lin_scatter = Plot.plot({
  width: 560, height: 250, marginLeft: 45,
  x: {label: "x (predictor)"}, y: {label: "y (response)", grid: true},
  marks: [
    lin_resid ? Plot.link(lin_data.pts, {x1: "x", x2: "x", y1: "y", y2: "fitted", stroke: red, strokeOpacity: 0.5}) : null,
    Plot.dot(lin_data.pts, {x: "x", y: "y", fill: grey, r: 3}),
    Plot.line(lin_data.pts.slice().sort((a, b) =&gt; a.x - b.x), {x: "x", y: "fitted", stroke: red, strokeWidth: 2.5})
  ]
})

lin_resplot = Plot.plot({
  width: 275, height: 210, marginLeft: 45,
  x: {label: "Fitted values"}, y: {label: "Residuals", grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: dark, strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
    Plot.dot(lin_data.pts, {x: "fitted", y: "resid", fill: grey, r: 2.5}),
    Plot.line(smooth(lin_data.pts.map((d) =&gt; d.fitted), lin_data.pts.map((d) =&gt; d.resid)), {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: red, strokeWidth: 2})
  ]
})

lin_qq = Plot.plot({
  width: 275, height: 210, marginLeft: 45,
  x: {label: "Theoretical quantiles"}, y: {label: "Standardised residuals", grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.line([[-2.6, -2.6], [2.6, 2.6]], {stroke: red, strokeWidth: 2}),
    Plot.dot(qqpoints(lin_data.pts.map((d) =&gt; d.std)), {x: "theoretical", y: "sample", fill: grey, r: 2.5})
  ]
})

html`&lt;div&gt;${lin_scatter}&lt;div class="app-row"&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;div class="app-plot-label"&gt;Residuals vs fitted&lt;/div&gt;${lin_resplot}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;div class="app-plot-label"&gt;Normal Q-Q plot of residuals&lt;/div&gt;${lin_qq}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="app-verdict"&gt;R&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; = &lt;b&gt;${fmt(lin_data.fit.r2)}&lt;/b&gt;, slope = &lt;b&gt;${fmt(lin_data.fit.b1)}&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;i&gt;p&lt;/i&gt; ${fmtp(lin_data.fit.p)}.
${{up: "A good fit: residuals are a flat cloud and the Q-Q points sit on the line.",
   down: "A good fit: the direction of the slope does not matter, only the pattern of the residuals.",
   curve: "A poor fit: the residuals form a U-shape. The relationship is curved, so a straight line is the wrong model.",
   fan: "A poor fit: the residuals spread out as fitted values increase (unequal variance). A log transformation or a GLM may help.",
   outlier: "One point drags the line and creates a long tail in the Q-Q plot. Check whether it is a data entry error."}[lin_type]}&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;`

1. Background
Multiple regression adds more predictors to the same equation:
\[
y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_{1i} + \beta_2 x_{2i} + \dots + \varepsilon_i
\]
Each slope is now a partial effect: the change in y for a one-unit change in that predictor while all other predictors are held constant. This lets you separate the effects of predictors that would otherwise be tangled together, for example temperature and fishing.
Predictors can be numbers or categories. For a categorical predictor such as fishing_allowed, the coefficient is the difference in mean y between that category and the reference category (here “No”).
Collinearity is the big trap. When two predictors are strongly correlated with each other (e.g. chlorophyll is high close to land and low far from land), the model cannot tell which one is responsible. The estimates become unstable: standard errors grow, slopes can flip sign, and neither predictor may look significant even though together they explain a lot (Dormann et al., 2013). The variance inflation factor (VIF) measures this. VIF &lt; 5 is fine, 5–10 is worrying and &gt; 10 is serious.


App: what collinearity does to your estimates





Code
viewof col_r = Inputs.range([0, 0.98], {value: 0.3, step: 0.02, label: "Correlation between predictors x1 and x2"})
viewof col_n = Inputs.range([20, 200], {value: 50, step: 10, label: "Sample size per study"})
viewof col_seed = Inputs.button("Run 300 new studies")



























Code
col_sims = {
  const r = rng(909 + col_seed);
  const out = [];
  for (let s = 0; s &lt; 300; s++) {
    const x1 = rnorm(r, col_n), e = rnorm(r, col_n), noise = rnorm(r, col_n);
    const x2 = x1.map((v, i) =&gt; col_r * v + Math.sqrt(1 - col_r ** 2) * e[i]);
    const y = x1.map((v, i) =&gt; 1 + 0.5 * v + 0.5 * x2[i] + noise[i]);
    out.push(lm2(x1, x2, y).b1);
  }
  return out;
}

Plot.plot({
  width: 560, height: 270, marginLeft: 45,
  x: {label: "Estimated slope of x1 in each simulated study", domain: [-1.5, 2.5]},
  y: {label: "Number of studies", grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.rectY(col_sims, Plot.binX({y: "count"}, {x: (d) =&gt; Math.max(-1.5, Math.min(2.49, d)), thresholds: Array.from({length: 41}, (_, i) =&gt; -1.5 + i * 0.1), fill: grey, inset: 0.5})),
    Plot.ruleX([0.5], {stroke: red, strokeWidth: 3}),
    Plot.ruleX([0], {stroke: dark, strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
    Plot.ruleY([0])
  ]
})
















Code
html`&lt;div class="app-verdict"&gt;The true slope is 0.5 (red line). Spread of the estimates (SD) = &lt;b&gt;${fmt(sd(col_sims))}&lt;/b&gt;.
VIF = 1 / (1 − r²) = &lt;b&gt;${fmt(1 / (1 - col_r ** 2), 1)}&lt;/b&gt;.
${col_r &gt; 0.9 ? "With strongly correlated predictors the estimates are all over the place, and some studies even get the sign wrong (left of the dashed line)."
  : "With weakly correlated predictors most studies estimate the slope close to its true value."}
Increase the correlation and watch the histogram spread out.&lt;/div&gt;`

1. Background

Probability and odds
For a binary response we model the probability (p) that the outcome is 1 (e.g. that the frog is present). Probabilities are stuck between 0 and 1, so a straight line does not work: it will eventually predict probabilities below 0 or above 1. Instead, logistic regression models the log-odds:
\[
\text{odds} = \frac{p}{1-p} \qquad\qquad \log\left(\frac{p_i}{1-p_i}\right) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i
\]

Odds compare the chance of “yes” with the chance of “no”. p = 0.5 gives odds of 1 (1:1), p = 0.8 gives odds of 4 (4:1).
The logit link, log(odds), turns a probability between 0 and 1 into a number between −∞ and +∞, so a straight line is fine on that scale.
On the probability scale the line becomes an S-shaped curve that never goes below 0 or above 1.
\(e^{\beta_1}\) is the odds ratio: how much the odds are multiplied for every one-unit increase in x. An odds ratio &gt; 1 means p increases with x, &lt; 1 means it decreases, and 1 means no effect.

The response follows a binomial distribution (for 0/1 data, also called the Bernoulli distribution), so in R we use glm(..., family = binomial).
Use the app to see how the intercept and slope control the S-curve, and why a straight line (lm()) fails for binary data.


App: the logistic curve





Code
import {rng, runif, logistic, lm1, fmt, red, grey, dark, blue} from "./apps/ecs200.js"









Code
viewof lg_b0 = Inputs.range([-6, 6], {value: -2.5, step: 0.1, label: "Intercept β₀ (log-odds when x = 0)"})
viewof lg_b1 = Inputs.range([-0.15, 0.15], {value: 0.06, step: 0.005, label: "Slope β₁ (change in log-odds per unit of x)"})
viewof lg_lm = Inputs.toggle({label: "Also show a straight line (lm)", value: false})
viewof lg_seed = Inputs.button("Resample")


































Code
lg_data = {
  const r = rng(1111 + lg_seed);
  const x = runif(r, 80, 0, 100);
  const pts = x.map((v) =&gt; {
    const p = logistic(lg_b0 + lg_b1 * v);
    const y = r() &lt; p ? 1 : 0;
    return {x: v, y, yj: y + (r() - 0.5) * 0.06};
  });
  const curve = Array.from({length: 101}, (_, i) =&gt; ({x: i, p: logistic(lg_b0 + lg_b1 * i)}));
  const fit = lm1(pts.map((d) =&gt; d.x), pts.map((d) =&gt; d.y));
  return {pts, curve, fit};
}

Plot.plot({
  width: 560, height: 320, marginLeft: 50,
  x: {label: "Predictor x (e.g. vegetation cover, %)", domain: [0, 100]},
  y: {label: "Probability of presence", domain: [-0.25, 1.25], ticks: [0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1], tickFormat: ".2f", grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleY([0, 1], {stroke: dark}),
    Plot.dot(lg_data.pts, {x: "x", y: "yj", fill: grey, fillOpacity: 0.6, r: 3}),
    Plot.line(lg_data.curve, {x: "x", y: "p", stroke: red, strokeWidth: 3}),
    lg_lm ? Plot.line([[0, lg_data.fit.b0], [100, lg_data.fit.b0 + 100 * lg_data.fit.b1]], {stroke: blue, strokeWidth: 2.5, strokeDasharray: "6,4"}) : null
  ]
})
















