Common statistical mistakes
Most mistakes in published research are not failures of advanced mathematics. They are everyday errors of design and interpretation that are easy to make and easy to miss (Makin and Orban de Xivry, 2019). Each tab below has an app: move the sliders, press Resample and watch how the conclusion can change, often dramatically, from nothing more than chance, sample size or a decision made by the researcher. The apps were inspired by BIOL 3P96 Biostatistics.

ControlsFake correlationConfoundersNon-significant results



Mistaking a change over time for a real treatment effect.

Does mining increase feral cat activity (e.g. because of more human activity)? A before–after, control–impact (BACI) design compares four groups:

Control before: the reference (unmined) site before mining started.
Control after: the reference site after mining started elsewhere.
Impact before: the mine site before mining.
Impact after: the mine site after mining.




A feral cat recorded on a camera trap. Image from Hohnen et al. (2021), NESP Threatened Species Recovery Hub Project 8.4.1 report.


Cat activity can change over time for reasons that have nothing to do with mining (e.g. season or rainfall). Only the control site tells you how big that background change is.





